# Reflection
Building this Student Event Registration Formatter across three versions taught me how helpful step-by-step development and GitHub version control can be. Starting with basic input and output in Version 1 let me make sure my variables worked properly before adding string formatting in Version 2 and date formatting in Version 3. Seeing the commit history show my progress made it much easier to organize my work and keep track of every improvement.
I also learned how built-in string methods and date/time features make user input much cleaner and easier to read. Because users might type names or event titles in all lowercase or with extra spaces, using `.strip()`, `.title()`, and `.upper()` keeps the final summary neat and consistent. At the same time, Python's `datetime` module turns plain numbers like `2026`, `6`, and `3` into a clear, readable date like `June 3, 2026`.
One challenge I faced was making sure the numeric inputs for the year, month, and day worked without errors when passed into the `date()` function. I solved this by converting those inputs into integers using `int()` before formatting them with `.strftime('%B')`. Overall, this activity showed me how small formatting tools can turn messy inputs into a clear and reliable summary.
